{
  "teamId": 146,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Miami Marlins were a team in full rebuild mode, a franchise stripped to its studs after the fire sale that followed the 2017 World Series championship and the tragic death of José Fernández. With a payroll near the bottom of the league and a roster built largely on journeymen, prospects, and castoffs, Miami entered the season with modest expectations — and the results mostly matched them. They finished 57-105, the second-worst record in baseball, surrendering 808 runs against only 615 scored.\n\nApril set the tone cruelly. After a modest 2-2 start in March, the Marlins cratered to 6-19 in April, with Miguel Rojas's double-play opening day blunder symbolizing a month where nothing went right. The brief rays of light — Jorge Alfaro's go-ahead homer, Curtis Granderson adding a blast of his own, Starlin Castro's clutch shot late in the month — were overwhelmed by the wave of losses crashing over Marlins Park. The pitching staff was battered, the offense was anemic, and the gap between Miami and the rest of the National League East was already yawning wide.\n\nMay and June offered something Miami desperately needed: fleeting hope. Garrett Cooper's walk-off grand slam on May 23rd was the season's single most uplifting moment by win probability, a thunderclap in an otherwise quiet summer. Martín Prado came through with a clutch double in June, and JT Riddle added a timely home run. The Marlins actually went 11-15 in May and 13-14 in June — nearly breaking even — suggesting that even a stripped-down roster could compete on individual nights. But for every Cooper grand slam, Juan Soto or Ronald Acuña delivered a gut-punch homer on the other side.\n\nThe second half of the season was a slow dissolution. July brought a 9-15 record, August cratered to a brutal 7-22 — the worst month of the year — as Nolan Arenado and Anthony Rendon torched the bullpen in memorable fashion. September offered a few last gasps of dignity: Starlin Castro hitting number 15, Miguel Rojas driving in multiple runs with a clutch double, and Austin Dean and Jon Berti providing late-season highlights for a young roster finding its footing. But the final chapter was written in losses, including José Ureña getting burned twice in the final weeks. The 2019 Marlins were not a team trying to win — they were a team trying to survive, to develop, and to lay the groundwork for something better down the road.",
